== Course Description

Economists ask a big question, "What choices should we make when we can't have everything?" Each of us make hundreds of choices every day as an individual and as a member of a larger social, economic, and political environment. This class is about this question, examining individual choices and how choices interact in groups. Tools in this class organize your thinking about decisions we face as individuals and as a society.

In Part A we set the table for the semester by building a model of cooperation, production, and shared benefit. In Part B we examine how markets can coordinate cooperation across large groups of decision-makers. In Part C we introduce externalities and other ways markets fail, along with some policy tools to correct the problems. In Part D we study strategic interaction: games, the commons, public goods, and voting. In Part E we consider how sellers' decisions generate the supply curve, examining market power and decision dynamics. In Part F we close out the semester by building a model of buyers' choices, generating the demand curve.

Economics relies heavily on mathematics as a cognitive step-stool. Proficiency in algebra is helpful for this class. But no matter who you are or where you're coming from, if you're willing to work hard, you are more than capable of excelling in the class. Welcome!

== Logistics

Classes meet in David Lawrence 120 on most Mondays and Wednesdays at 12PM an 1PM. Classes introduce new ideas, work through exercises, run simulations, and offer Checkpoints. Your TA will facilitate guided practice in your recitation on Thursday or Friday. These recitations~are a low-stakes environment for longer practice sessions in a smaller groups of students broken out from the lecture. Then we’ll send you off to finish studying and work~on your~homework with your classmates.~Economics is best learned through practice, so put in the time and reps to master it.

Core course resources include:

- *Teaching Assistants* are highly trained economists in the graduate department who are 1) your first person of contact with questions, and 2) facilitate weekly recitations. Do not hesitate to be in touch with your TA.
- *Course Website* holds the class together, where you'll find:
  
  - *The Skillsheet* lists every assessed skill your grade is built from.
  - *Videos*~cover core content.
  - *Homework* for your practice.
  - *Demos*~as extra practice problem videos for the Checkpoints.
  - *Office Hours* are a great resource. Come see me or your TA!
- *Canvas*~records your passed skills and my announcements.
- *Gradescope* will be the portal to collect and return assignments.

- *The Textbook* for the class is OpenStax's _Principles of Microeconomics_ (3rd edition) — a free, openly licensed textbook you can read on the web or download as a PDF at https://openstax.org. A copy is also posted on the course page. If you'd like a second reference, Gregory Mankiw's _Principles of Microeconomics_ is excellent, and used copies of any edition are inexpensive. Let me know if you have questions.

== Grading

You belong in my classroom no matter your grade in the class. I've deliberately designed the class to push you to do excellent work and give you the best opportunity to succeed. In return I expect you to work hard.

Your Final Grade is the percentage of assessed *skills* that you pass/no-pass on *Checkpoints *and* Reattempts*. Checkpoints and Reattempts allow you to save your progress in the class: skills you pass stay passed; and if you no-pass a skill on an in-class Checkpoint, you can try again during a Reattempt. The assessed skills are those that appear on a Checkpoint this semester and are recorded on the *Skillsheet* with their posted standard. If you meet the standard, you pass the skill. For example, if you pass 94% of the skills, your final grade is a 94%. Letter grades follow the standard breaks:

