Below 60% is an F. There is no curve: your grade does not depend on anyone else’s grade. Everyone can get an A, and I'd be thrilled!

There will be one Checkpoint for each of the six parts: Parts A-E in class roughly every other week (dates in the schedule below) and Part F during the Final Exam Period. Your study of a skill does not end at the Checkpoint. Reattempt sessions will be offered~throughout the semester (timing and logistics will be announced on Canvas). Reattempts can only help you: all skills you’ve passed stay passed. A reattempt replaces your first attempt _ only_~_in your favor_.~You only redo the skills you haven't yet passed.

The Final Exam Period will include Checkpoint F and a reattempt session.~

During Checkpoints and Reattempts

+ You may use material from your notes and the textbook.
+ You may not use any electronic material other than a calculator.
+ Your exam should be your own and is subject to the Academic Conduct Code (below). If you are *at all unclear* about a particular piece of material with respect to these two guidelines, it is your responsibility to clarify its appropriateness with the instructor. This is taken very seriously.

=== Practice

Learning is done through study and practice.~Practice unlocks skills on the Checkpoints and Reattempts.~

- Complete~*2 of 3* types of practice for a skill to unlock a first attempt.
- Complete~*3 of 3* types of practice for a skill to unlock a reattempt.

Each skill has three types of practice:

+ *Exercises (due in class)*~are structured practice done _together~in class_.
+ *Vignettes (due in rec.)*~are guided practice done _together~in recitation_.
+ *Homework (due on Sundays)*~is practice done on _your own time_.

Practice is graded for completion not correctness. You’re allowed to make mistakes. Group work on homework is strongly encouraged. I'd go as far as to say that if you're not working in a group, you're not doing this class right. Teach and learn from your classmates.

You may sit for a Checkpoint even if you have not unlocked every skill. Simply~answer the skills you_ have_~unlocked on schedule. Practice cannot be made up before a Checkpoint. But you may make up any of your missed practice after the Checkpoint during the next Reattempt.

== Disability Services

If I were a student at Pitt today I would qualify for accommodations and would use those resources. If you have a disability for which you are or may be requesting an accommodation, you are encouraged to contact both your instructor and Disability Resources and Services (DRS), 140 William Pitt Union, (412) 648-7890, drsrecep\@pitt.edu, (412) 228-5347 for P3 ASL users, as early as possible in the term. DRS will verify your disability and determine reasonable accommodations for this course.

== Email Policy

Don't hesitate to email me or your TA with questions. When you do, be considerate of a few things. First, I will try to respond to you as quickly as possible. It's easiest for me to respond if your email has a direct question. I get a considerable number of emails, so during busy times it may take longer than a day or two to get back to you. Second, I do not check emails outside of regular working hours, so if you email me at night or on the weekend, I likely won't see your email until later. This is a good practice I'd encourage for you too.

== AI Policy

Artificial intelligence can be a powerful tool and something you will need to be familiar with in your life and work. This class does not prohibit use of AI. But keep the design of the class in mind: your grade comes entirely from skills you demonstrate yourself on paper in class. Practice is for you to develop those skills. If you simply hand your practice to an AI to do for you, you'll feel it on the Checkpoints. I would encourage you to treat Homework like a Checkpoint, doing it without the direct aid of AI. If you find a clever use of AI, particularly for studying or note creation, I'd love to hear about it!

== Academic Conduct Code

All students are expected to adhere to the standards of the University of Pittsburgh's Policy on Academic Integrity. Any violation is a serious matter. Any student engaged in cheating, plagiarism, or other acts of academic dishonesty would be subject to disciplinary action, and may 1) fail all skills assessed on the Checkpoint in question with no reattempt, or 2) receive an F in the class.

